<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!-- The device has no ambient-light or proximity sensor hardware.
         Both of these restate the AOSP default (config.xml:754 and :1139) and
         are kept as an explicit statement of fact about this chassis. -->
    <bool name="config_automatic_brightness_available">false</bool>
    <bool name="config_suspendWhenScreenOffDueToProximity">false</bool>

    <!-- There is no wake-up/significant-motion sensor.
         DeviceIdleController.java:3568-3578 makes scheduleAlarmLocked() return
         without scheduling ANYTHING when mUseMotionSensor is true and
         mMotionSensor is null, with AOSP's own comment saying that "effectively
         turns off normal execution of device idling". That is the 32-hours-in-
         light-idle symptom this was measured against.

         What the false value actually buys, stated correctly because an earlier
         revision of this comment got it wrong: with the bool false,
         stepIdleStateLocked takes the else at :3109-3120 and sets
         mNotMoving = true unconditionally, so there is NO motion gate on the
         deep-idle path at all. It is not "the stationary timeout path instead";
         startMonitoringMotionLocked() at :3503 is a no-op either way, because
         mMotionSensor is null in both configurations and mStationaryListeners
         therefore never arms. Deep Doze on this chassis is time-based only. -->
    <bool name="config_autoPowerModeUseMotionSensor">false</bool>

    <!-- Wired-headset detection. There is no 3.5 mm jack; the USB-C port
         carries ANALOG audio accessory mode, so the headset is wired to the
         internal codec through the Type-C mux and the PMIC's ACCDET block
         detects it exactly as it would a jack. This kernel exposes that only as
         EV_SW on the "ACCDET" input device: it has no /sys/class/switch/h2w and
         no extcon node, so WiredAccessoryManager's two other sources
         (WiredAccessoryManager.java:430-438 for h2w, :131 for extcon) find
         nothing. The switch path is gated on this resource
         (WiredAccessoryManager.java:107-108, :115-130), which AOSP defaults
         false.

         Measured with the owner's USB-C headset in the port (E-088):
         "dumpsys input" reports SwitchValues: 94 on device ACCDET, and that
         field is printed with %x (SwitchInputMapper::dump), so it is 0x94 =
         SW_HEADPHONE_INSERT | SW_MICROPHONE_INSERT | SW_JACK_PHYSICAL_INSERT.
         The kernel sees the headset; only the framework did not.

         An earlier revision asserted "DO NOT set it true on this chassis",
         reasoning from a live check that reported SwitchValues: 0. That check
         was run with nothing plugged in, so it measured an empty port and not
         the absence of the hardware.

         This gates EV_SW only. It does not re-enable the ACCDET inline-remote
         key events, which stay neutralised in keylayout/ACCDET.kl for a
         separate and still-valid reason. -->
    <bool name="config_useDevInputEventForAudioJack">true</bool>

    <!-- Punch-hole front camera, measured on the handset against a 1:1
         720x1560 calibration target (tools/cutout/): the opaque aperture,
         including its black ring, spans x 330..388 and y 12..70 display pixels
         - a 58 px circle centred at (359, 41), one pixel left of the display
         centre line.

         Pixels, not @dp: the path has no @dp marker, so DisplayCutout takes the
         coordinates verbatim with the origin at the top centre of the display.
         @dp would scale by ro.sf.lcd_density/160 and ignore any runtime `wm
         density` override, which is wrong for a physical aperture. Do not use
         @ratio here either - it is Android 11+, and on Q it makes PathParser
         throw and the whole cutout is dropped behind a Log.wtf.

         The drawn path is two half arcs plus a degenerate subpath at the origin
         so its bounding box starts at y=0, as AOSP's own emulation overlays do.
         The RectApproximation is what the framework actually measures: apps see
         safeInsetTop = its bounding-box bottom = 70 px. -->
    <string translatable="false" name="config_mainBuiltInDisplayCutout">
        M -30,41
        A 29,29 0 1,0 28,41
        A 29,29 0 1,0 -30,41
        Z
        M 0,0 L 0,0 Z
    </string>
    <string translatable="false" name="config_mainBuiltInDisplayCutoutRectApproximation">
        M -30,0
        L -30,70
        L 28,70
        L 28,0
        Z
    </string>

    <!-- Let SystemUI paint the aperture black so its edge is anti-aliased
         against the physical hole. -->
    <bool name="config_fillMainBuiltInDisplayCutout">true</bool>

    <!-- Keep the full 720x1560 logical display. true would permanently
         letterbox the cutout strip away and hide it from apps entirely. -->
    <bool name="config_maskMainBuiltInDisplayCutout">false</bool>

    <bool name="config_showNavigationBar">true</bool>

    <!-- Near-ultrasound. AOSP defaults BOTH of these to true
         (frameworks/base/core/res/res/values/config.xml:3244 and :3247), which
         is a CDD claim that the microphones capture and the speaker emits
         usable signal in the 18.5-20 kHz band. They are surfaced to apps as
         AudioManager.PROPERTY_SUPPORT_MIC_NEAR_ULTRASOUND and
         PROPERTY_SUPPORT_SPEAKER_NEAR_ULTRASOUND and are used for ultrasonic
         pairing and presence detection.

         Nothing here has ever measured it, and the hardware is a single bottom
         speaker plus two MEMS microphones on a shanzhai MT6755 chassis whose
         audio path this project has already found to be shipping stock
         pre-processing UUIDs against the wrong library. This tree's rule is that
         a declared capability needs evidence, so both go false: an app that
         believes the claim and fails is worse than one that never tries.

         Revisit only with a real sweep (play a 19 kHz tone through the
         loudspeaker and record it on each mic), not by restoring the default. -->
    <bool name="config_supportMicNearUltrasound">false</bool>
    <bool name="config_supportSpeakerNearUltrasound">false</bool>

    <!-- config_defaultPeakRefreshRate is deliberately NOT overridden. AOSP Q
         already defaults it to 60 (frameworks/base/core/res/res/values/
         config.xml:4158; readers DisplayModeDirector.java:572 and :599) and this
         panel reports exactly one mode, 720x1560 @ 60.0. An override that
         restates a default is a line a future reader has to verify for nothing.

         And a stronger reason to keep this file free of them: framework-res is
         built with the aapt2 auto-add-overlay flag
         (frameworks/base/core/res/Android.bp:34-35),
         so a MISSPELLED resource name here is silently ADDED rather than
         rejected. Every name in this file has to be checked by hand against
         upstream; the fewer there are, the likelier that stays true. -->

    <!-- Night Light. AOSP derives config_nightDisplayAvailable from
         config_setColorTransformAccelerated (config.xml:1009), which defaults
         false, so without this override the device has NO blue-light filter at
         all: LiveDisplay is off for the same reason and `dumpsys color_display`
         reported "Night display: Not available".

         config_setColorTransformAccelerated is deliberately LEFT false, because
         it means specifically "setColorTransform can be done efficiently in
         HARDWARE" and on this HWC it cannot. Measured on the handset by turning
         on accessibility colour inversion, which drives the same
         DisplayTransformManager path: every layer in `dumpsys SurfaceFlinger`
         h/w composer state flipped DEV -> CLI. That is the HWC2 contract for a
         composer that cannot apply the matrix itself, and it is what makes the
         feature work: SurfaceFlinger.cpp:3475 computes
         applyColorMatrix = !hasDeviceComposition && !skipClientColorTransform,
         so with no device composition left RenderEngine applies the matrix. The
         panel held 63 fps throughout.

         Keeping the accelerated bool false is not cosmetic. It keeps colour
         inversion and colour correction in Settings' Experimental category with
         AOSP's own "may affect performance" footer
         (AccessibilitySettings.java:736, ToggleDaltonizerPreferenceFragment.java:65),
         which is the truth here, and it leaves the developer-only global
         saturation control unavailable (GlobalSaturationTintController.java:79).

         Cost, stated rather than discovered later: while Night Light is on the
         whole screen is GPU-composited, and `dumpsys SurfaceFlinger` reports
         "RenderEngine supports protected context: 0", so DRM-protected video may
         blank for as long as it is on. Both are user-reversible from the same
         toggle. -->
    <bool name="config_nightDisplayAvailable">true</bool>

    <!-- The chip is dual-band and AOSP's default for this is false. `iw list`
         on the handset enumerates a Band 2 (5180/5200/5220/5240 MHz at 30 dBm,
         plus the DFS 5260-5700 set) and lists AP among the supported interface
         modes on the same phy; the handset is associated on 5 GHz right now.
         WifiServiceImpl.isDualBandSupported() reads this resource, and
         Settings' WifiTetherApBandPreferenceController hides the hotspot band
         picker when it is false - so leaving it false pins SoftAP to 2.4 GHz
         with no way to change it. -->
    <bool name="config_wifi_dual_band_support">true</bool>

    <!-- Hardware-offloaded PNO. AOSP defaults this false (config.xml:576) and
         its only reader, WificondScannerImpl.java:110, then runs every
         screen-off saved-network scan on the host CPU instead of in the Wi-Fi
         firmware.

         True because the shipped driver implements it, not because other trees
         set it: wlan_drv_gen2.ko exports mtk_cfg80211_sched_scan_start and
         mtk_cfg80211_sched_scan_stop and emits cfg80211_sched_scan_results /
         cfg80211_sched_scan_stopped, i.e. the full NL80211_CMD_START_SCHED_SCAN
         path.

         The failure mode if that is wrong is specific and observable: screen-off
         auto-reconnect stops working. Check with
             dumpsys wifi | grep numPnoScan
         after a screen-off window away from a known network -
         numPnoScanStartedOverOffload should be non-zero with
         numPnoScanFailedOverOffload at zero. All four counters read 0 on the
         predecessor image because it never disconnected while screen-off, so
         this needs a deliberate window to measure. -->
    <bool name="config_wifi_background_scan_support">true</bool>

    <!-- Tethering. AOSP ships all three of these arrays EMPTY
         (frameworks/base/core/res/res/values/config.xml:380-399), and
         Tethering.hasTetherableConfiguration() is false when they all are, so
         isTetheringSupported() returns false and Settings hides the entire
         "Hotspot & tethering" screen. On the flashed build the screen contained
         only "Allow clients to use VPNs" and `dumpsys connectivity` logged
         "wlan0 is not a tetherable iface, ignoring". No hotspot, no USB
         tethering, no Bluetooth PAN, on a device that has the hardware and
         the software for all three.

         Everything each one needs is already present and was checked:
           wifi  - /vendor/bin/hw/hostapd ships, android.hardware.wifi.hostapd
                   @1.0/@1.1 are declared in manifest.xml and served lazily, and
                   config_wifi_dual_band_support above is true. AOSP's
                   wifi_chip.cpp names AP interfaces from the same wlanN pool as
                   STA, so wlan\\d is the operative entry; softap\\d and ap\\d
                   are there because MTK vendor HALs have used both.
           usb   - rootdir/etc/init/hw/init.mt6755.usb.rc:50-62 already
                   implements the whole sys.usb.config=rndis path against the
                   legacy android_usb gadget (CONFIG_USB_G_ANDROID=y), which
                   names the interface rndis0.
           bt    - bt-pan is the AOSP/BlueDroid PAN interface name.

         config_wifi_dual_band_support being true without these was a half
         mechanism: the band the hotspot could not use was declared, and the
         hotspot itself could never start. -->
    <string-array translatable="false" name="config_tether_wifi_regexs">
        <item>"wlan\\d"</item>
        <item>"softap\\d"</item>
        <item>"ap\\d"</item>
    </string-array>
    <string-array translatable="false" name="config_tether_usb_regexs">
        <item>"rndis\\d"</item>
    </string-array>
    <string-array translatable="false" name="config_tether_bluetooth_regexs">
        <item>"bt-pan"</item>
    </string-array>

    <!-- Two physical SIM slots. AOSP defaults this to 1, and the device works
         anyway only because UiccController clamps it up to mCis.length ; state
         the fact rather than depend on the clamp. -->
    <integer name="config_num_physical_slots">2</integer>

    <!-- Physical 2SIM+SD tray. AOSP defaults this false, which makes
         UiccSlot.onIccSwap prompt a reboot on every insert/remove
         (UiccSlot.java). The default is for soldered/eSIM-primary devices.
         This chassis has a user-removable UICC tray; MediaTek's RIL already
         publishes CARDSTATE on insert/remove. true skips the reboot dialog
         and lets the modem/RIL handle the swap, which is AOSP Q practice for
         a tray phone. -->
    <bool name="config_hotswapCapable">true</bool>

    <!-- Bind the Android 10 MTK MMTEL service through AOSP ImsResolver. Voice
         over LTE and voice over Wi-Fi are device capabilities; carrier config
         and provisioning remain the second gate. Video telephony is omitted -
         it needs ccci_imsv/ccci_imsdc, which this tree deliberately leaves
         unlabelled, and libvt_avsync.so, which is not shipped. -->
    <bool name="config_dynamic_bind_ims">true</bool>
    <string name="config_ims_package" translatable="false">com.mediatek.ims</string>
    <bool name="config_device_volte_available">true</bool>
    <bool name="config_device_vt_available">false</bool>

    <!-- Wi-Fi calling. ImsManager.isWfcEnabledByPlatform() ANDs exactly this
         with KEY_CARRIER_WFC_IMS_AVAILABLE_BOOL and isGbaValid(). The filtered
         CarrierConfig overlay supplies the carrier half only for supported
         home identities; the current Chinese SIMs correctly remain false.
         Both device and carrier halves are needed; either alone does nothing.

         Do NOT use persist.dbg.wfc_avail_ovr. It short-circuits the whole AND
         before it is evaluated, which is the mistake the VoLTE gate already
         made once with persist.dbg.volte_avail_ovr.

         WFC is a CAPABILITY inside MMTEL (CAPABILITY_TYPE_VOICE x
         REGISTRATION_TECH_IWLAN), not a separate ImsFeature, so nothing about
         ImsResolver changes. ro.telephony.iwlan_operation_mode stays `legacy`:
         MediaTek's ePDG tunnel is a userspace strongSwan tunnel on wlan0, not
         an Android data transport, so no IQualifiedNetworksService is involved.

         config_carrier_wfc_ims_available is a DIFFERENT resource that exists in
         Android 10 and has zero Java readers. Overlaying it does nothing. -->
    <bool name="config_device_wfc_ims_available">true</bool>

    <!-- The device configurator, i.e. the one package allowed to write
         DeviceConfig. WRITE_DEVICE_CONFIG is
         protectionLevel="signature|verifier|configurator"
         (frameworks/base/core/res/AndroidManifest.xml:2624) and the
         configurator half is granted to exactly the package this resource
         names (PermissionManagerService.java:2050-2055 via
         PackageManagerService.getDeviceConfiguratorPackageName():21501-21504).
         AOSP leaves it empty (config.xml:2954), so on a GMS build nothing may
         write DeviceConfig and every GMS Phenotype flag push fails: measured
         at 11 344 W/PlatformConfigurator lines in one 14 h uptime, the single
         largest log producer on the handset, and zero afterwards.

         Correct only while the GMS payload is inherited. It is set here rather
         than in vendor/gapps because vendor/gapps is a generated mirror of
         MindTheGapps and is never hand-edited; the decision to ship GMS is the
         device's, and it is made ten lines apart in
         lineage_k50sv1_64_bsp.mk. Remove both together. -->
    <string name="config_deviceConfiguratorPackageName" translatable="false">com.google.android.gms</string>

    <!-- The PowerHAL scenario table implements SUSTAINED_PERFORMANCE
         (configs/powerscntbl.xml), capping both clusters at an OPP the part
         can hold indefinitely, but AOSP defaults this resource to false
         (config.xml:3451) and therefore tells apps the mode does not exist.
         Its only reader is PowerManager.isSustainedPerformanceModeSupported()
         at PowerManager.java:1744-1747, and PowerManager.java:1735-1737
         documents that call as the check an application is expected to make
         before Window.setSustainedPerformanceMode(). A well-behaved app
         therefore never asks, and the scenario never fires.

         The plumbing behind the request is already unconditional:
         RootWindowContainer.java:725-729 sends
         PowerHint.SUSTAINED_PERFORMANCE whenever the aggregate window state
         changes, with no reference to this resource. This overlay only stops
         the framework from denying that the mode exists. -->
    <bool name="config_sustainedPerformanceModeSupported">true</bool>

    <!-- Haptic pulse lengths, sized from THIS motor's measured perceptual
         threshold. The first attempt at this was reverted because it was
         guessed and made two of the three weaker; both the measurement and the
         earlier mistake are recorded here so neither is repeated.

         HOW THE THRESHOLD WAS MEASURED. Durations were written straight to
         /sys/class/timed_output/vibrator/enable, three seconds apart, and the
         owner reported by hand:

             500 / 200 / 100 / 60 ms   clear
             40 ms                     slightly noticeable
             30 / 20 ms                only felt if you concentrate

         So this ERM needs about 60 ms to be unambiguous, 40 ms is marginal, and
         anything at or below 30 ms is effectively nothing.

         WHY AOSP'S DEFAULTS ARE TOO SHORT HERE. They are LRA numbers, and they
         are OFF/ON PAIRS, and the pulse that does the work is the LAST element:

             config_longPressVibePattern    0, 1, 20, 21   -> a 21 ms pulse
             config_virtualKeyVibePattern   0, 10, 20, 30  -> a 30 ms pulse
             config_keyboardTapVibePattern  40             -> a 40 ms pulse

         Against the scale above, 21 ms is imperceptible and 30 ms nearly so.
         Only the keyboard tap was reliably felt. (The earlier revision of this
         file misread 1 and 10 as the pulses, replaced all three with single
         pulses of 25/20/25 ms, and thereby cut virtual-key drive from 40 ms of
         total on-time to 20 and keyboard tap from 40 to 25. The owner reported
         the motor as dead, which it effectively was.)

         WHY SINGLE PULSES. AOSP's two-pulse shape is an LRA texture: an LRA
         starts and stops in a few milliseconds, so 10-off-20-on reads as a
         crisp double tick. A rotor cannot do that: it spends the first
         ~20 ms spinning up and coasts on the way down, so the leading pulse is
         wasted and the gap is filled by spin-down. One longer pulse is the
         honest shape for this hardware.

         WHERE THESE LAND, which is why getting them wrong is felt immediately:
         this device ships android.hardware.vibrator@1.0, whose AOSP impl
         returns UNSUPPORTED_OPERATION from perform(), so EVERY prebaked effect
         falls back to these arrays. VibratorService.systemReady maps
         EFFECT_CLICK to config_virtualKeyVibePattern and EFFECT_HEAVY_CLICK to
         config_longPressVibePattern. Confirmed live: `dumpsys vibrator` shows
         Prebaked{mEffectId=0, mFallback=true} from com.android.launcher3. The
         intensity settings cannot compensate either: with no amplitude control
         VibrationEffect.scale() leaves DEFAULT_AMPLITUDE alone, so pulse LENGTH
         is the only lever this device has. -->
    <!-- Long press / EFFECT_HEAVY_CLICK. Infrequent and should be definite, so
         it gets the first duration the owner called clear. -->
    <integer-array name="config_longPressVibePattern">
        <item>0</item>
        <item>60</item>
    </integer-array>
    <!-- Virtual key / EFFECT_CLICK, the common one. Between marginal (40) and
         clear (60): noticeable without being heavy on every button press. -->
    <integer-array name="config_virtualKeyVibePattern">
        <item>0</item>
        <item>50</item>
    </integer-array>
    <!-- THERE IS NO SEPARATE KEYBOARD-TAP PULSE ON ANDROID 10, and an earlier
         revision of this file was built on the belief that there is. It said
         config_keyboardTapVibePattern was "deliberately LEFT AT AOSP'S 40 ms"
         and reasoned about blurring consecutive keystrokes. That resource is
         DEAD in Q: it is declared at frameworks/base/core/res/res/values/
         config.xml:1241 with a java-symbol at symbols.xml:1714 and has ZERO
         Java readers in frameworks/, packages/, lineage-sdk/ or vendor/lineage.

         What actually happens: PhoneWindowManager.getVibrationEffect()
         (services/core/java/com/android/server/policy/PhoneWindowManager.java:
         6122-6127) maps KEYBOARD_TAP (the same constant as KEYBOARD_PRESS)
         together with VIRTUAL_KEY, EDGE_RELEASE, CONFIRM and
         GESTURE_START onto VibrationEffect.EFFECT_CLICK, and this vibrator@1.0
         has no perform(), so VibratorService falls back to
         config_virtualKeyVibePattern (VibratorService.java:379-380, :389).

         So config_virtualKeyVibePattern IS the keystroke pulse. The 50 ms above
         is what a keyboard tap fires at, and it is deliberately below the 60 ms
         "clear" mark for exactly the reason the old comment gave: this rotor's
         spin-down would blur consecutive keys. There is no second lever, and
         overriding config_keyboardTapVibePattern would change nothing.

         config_clockTickVibePattern is a real resource and is overridden below.
         config_calendarDateVibePattern is read directly by
         PhoneWindowManager:6134-6135 for CALENDAR_DATE and gets the same
         treatment for the same reason.

         config_safeModeEnabledVibePattern is left alone: its leading 1 ms has
         the same problem, but it is followed by a 500/600 ms pair that nothing
         could miss, and it fires once. -->

    <!-- EFFECT_TICK / EFFECT_TEXTURE_TICK, i.e. the light feedback. This one
         array drives eight haptic constants, not just the picker the earlier
         comment named: VibratorService.java:385-386 builds tickEffect from it,
         :391 and :394-395 map EFFECT_TICK and EFFECT_TEXTURE_TICK onto it, and
         PhoneWindowManager.java:6106-6120 routes CONTEXT_CLICK,
         TEXT_HANDLE_MOVE, CLOCK_TICK, KEYBOARD_RELEASE, VIRTUAL_KEY_RELEASE,
         ENTRY_BUMP, DRAG_CROSSING and GESTURE_END through those two.

         AOSP's 125, 30 is 125 ms of silence then a 30 ms pulse, and 30 ms is
         inside this motor's "only if you concentrate" band, so six of those
         eight one-shot constants currently produce nothing a hand can feel.

         KEEP THE 125 ms LEAD-IN. It is not padding: while a picker is flung,
         each new tick cancels the pending one before it fires, which is what
         stops a continuous buzz. Raising only the pulse lifts the one-shots to
         the measured "slightly noticeable" mark without touching that.

         40 rather than 60 on purpose. These are the LIGHT constants; a 60 ms
         pulse on a text-handle drag would read as a fault, not as feedback. -->
    <integer-array name="config_clockTickVibePattern">
        <item>125</item>
        <item>40</item>
    </integer-array>
    <integer-array name="config_calendarDateVibePattern">
        <item>125</item>
        <item>40</item>
    </integer-array>


</resources>
